  "decisions": {
    "source-abstract": {
      "status": "user-direction",
      "instruction": "Interpret the drawing; do not require literal source overlap or feature coordinates.",
      "words": "I don't think we should keep that rule in place because my images are always going to be an abstraction of what I actually want",
      "source": "Nick, this chat, 2026-09-27"
    },
    "ears-eyes": {
      "status": "user-direction",
      "instruction": "Ear edges are shaggy hair; eyes should be large and distinctive rather than literal squiggles.",
      "words": "the \"ragged edge ears\" are supposed to just be shaggy hair, not jagged edges. Regarding the eyes, the squiggle eyes that are there are more of an abstraction. So I just want the eyes to be something big and unique looking more than explicitly wanting you to have the jagged look. but sure lets see what you produce from these",
      "source": "Nick, this chat, 2026-09-27"
    },
    "style": {
      "status": "user-direction",
      "instruction": "The preferred first grayscale round is the primary whole-creature likeness target, with later scoped corrections retained.",
      "words": "Your first image generation, I liked much better than your second round",
      "source": "Nick, this chat, 2026-09-27"
    },
    "tail-root": {
      "status": "user-direction",
      "instruction": "One rear midline tail root at the base of spine.",
      "words": "yes The tail should be coming from the base of the spine, like every real animal's tail does",
      "source": "Nick, this chat, 2026-09-27",
      "wholeBodyCorrectionWords": "The back view went back to showing the tail connecting in a spot that was not centered above where it should be. It should be at the base of the spine, not offset to the left. Also, the butt is showing sexual again. You reduce the contours of the butt cheeks so that it does not look as sexual. Also, the creature's eyes look a bit cross-eyed. I like everything about the eye except for the fact that it's cross-eyed. Everything else looks good to me"
    },
    "tail-fork-pelvis": {
      "status": "user-direction",
      "instruction": "Three distinct tails fuse together and with the body at a compact spinal base. Remove any extended common trunk or delayed trident split. Preserve smooth continuity and restrained pelvis.",
      "words": "Think through it a little bit more. Your design is showing one base, but then there's two pieces that sort of branch out from mid-air behind the tail. It needs to have the three pieces of the tail form together more cleanly, like the base of a tree trunk does with its branches, rather than just having two more tails spawn out of the one tail that connects to the body. I also want you to tone down the butt form a tad because it's a little too sexual",
      "followUpWords": "yeah thats better, proceed",
      "source": "Nick, this chat, 2026-09-27",
      "latestCorrectionWords": "Yes, and I also think you need to fix where the tail splits. Right now it's shaped more like a trident where there's one base and it branches later. But what I want is more like three distinct tails coming to one base where they're all fusing together at the base and body together",
      "continuationWords": "proceed with adjustments",
      "wholeBodyCorrectionWords": "The back view went back to showing the tail connecting in a spot that was not centered above where it should be. It should be at the base of the spine, not offset to the left. Also, the butt is showing sexual again. You reduce the contours of the butt cheeks so that it does not look as sexual. Also, the creature's eyes look a bit cross-eyed. I like everything about the eye except for the fact that it's cross-eyed. Everything else looks good to me"
    },
    "modeling-pose": {
      "status": "proposal",
      "instruction": "Separate pose with arms down and clear of torso, feet modestly apart.",
      "approval": null
    },
    "tail-depth": {
      "status": "user-direction",
      "instruction": "Full rounded cattail-like tail bodies with near-circular cross sections and meaningful depth. Each narrows to a pointed tip. Exact dimensions remain provisional.",
      "words": "It should feel fuller what you built is too flat, It should look closer to three cattails than whatever fork-looking thing you have there",
      "source": "Nick, this chat, 2026-09-27",
      "approval": null
    },
    "tail-taper": {
      "status": "user-direction",
      "instruction": "Full rounded tails must narrow through sustained taper into pointed tips. Taper is part of construction geometry, not deferred to fur.",
      "questionWords": "What level should I be judging this? Obviously, I understand it's not the final product, but things like the fact that it feels like the tips of the tail aren't pointy enough. Is that something that is irrelevant for this layer ?",
      "confirmationWords": "Yes, and I also think you need to fix where the tail splits. Right now it's shaped more like a trident where there's one base and it branches later. But what I want is more like three distinct tails coming to one base where they're all fusing together at the base and body together",
      "continuationWords": "proceed with adjustments",
      "source": "Nick, this chat, 2026-09-27",
      "approval": null
    },
    "tail-shape-acceptance": {
      "status": "accepted-aspects",
      "instruction": "Probe-0003 tail shape is accepted. Preserve volume, taper and direct body fusion; pose is explicitly excluded.",
      "words": "Yeah, that's much better. I don't really have much to say to adjust it. The pose that it's in isn't necessarily my favorite, but the shape of the tail I think looks all right",
      "source": "Nick, this chat",
      "evidence": {
        "path": "docs/design/species-construction/akinza/tail-shape-acceptance.json",
        "role": "scoped-acceptance",
        "sha256": "d2cae7e50648bd2b384662b226403d2205ebe14fb4a1d1612f87540c1c4b7d1a"
      }
    },
    "tail-pose": {
      "status": "deferred",
      "instruction": "Nick declined A/B pose coaching. Agent chooses a neutral technical arrangement for construction. Expressive posing waits until the complete creature can be judged. Accepted tail form is unchanged.",
      "source": "Nick, this chat: no, Do I need to be coaching you on the pose here, or is that a later phase",
      "approval": null
    },
    "body-direction": {
      "status": "accepted-aspects",
      "instruction": "Carry forward remaining visible body and eye design, subject to centered spinal root, reduced rear contours and forward gaze corrections. Corrected outputs await review.",
      "words": "The back view went back to showing the tail connecting in a spot that was not centered above where it should be. It should be at the base of the spine, not offset to the left. Also, the butt is showing sexual again. You reduce the contours of the butt cheeks so that it does not look as sexual. Also, the creature's eyes look a bit cross-eyed. I like everything about the eye except for the fact that it's cross-eyed. Everything else looks good to me",
      "source": "Nick, this chat",
      "evidence": {
        "path": "docs/design/species-construction/akinza/body-direction-acceptance.json",
        "role": "scoped-acceptance",
        "sha256": "f2133cf4300db8fc555bc6670f339e70f5336c8c2e262f41301041d7b7bab600"
      }
    },
    "body-details": {
      "status": "partially-superseded",
      "instruction": "Retain study-0005 ear-shell interpretation only; study-0010 gaze and study-0020 animal paws supersede the other panels. Exact biological digit count remains unratified.",
      "approval": null
    },
    "forward-gaze": {
      "status": "user-direction",
      "instruction": "Preserve large distinctive oval eye design, but center pupils for forward parallel gaze without inward convergence.",
      "words": "The back view went back to showing the tail connecting in a spot that was not centered above where it should be. It should be at the base of the spine, not offset to the left. Also, the butt is showing sexual again. You reduce the contours of the butt cheeks so that it does not look as sexual. Also, the creature's eyes look a bit cross-eyed. I like everything about the eye except for the fact that it's cross-eyed. Everything else looks good to me",
      "source": "Nick, this chat"
    },
    "gaze-acceptance": {
      "status": "accepted-aspects",
      "instruction": "Study-0010 forward gaze accepted; later rear correction does not revoke the gaze.",
      "words": "yup",
      "source": "Nick, this chat, with subsequent rear-only withdrawal",
      "evidence": {
        "path": "docs/design/species-construction/akinza/correction-review.json",
        "role": "feedback-sequence",
        "sha256": "97597f149f1310f67b47a6251c2763a18acf00fa3405f7f4669127844f5a6df0"
      }
    },
    "root-bilateral-overlap": {
      "status": "user-direction",
      "instruction": "One compact shared root at tailbone, centered across the rear midline, overlapping some left and right rear body surface; three tails fuse at exactly this same body-level site before sweeping left",
      "words": "I think you need to do some online research to look at what tails look like on animals because I should not be able to see the entire right buttocks since the tail fusion should happen directly center. So it should be overlapping a little bit on the left butux and the right Botox, but you have it all on the left cheek",
      "priorClarificationWords": "Actually, no, the rear view still shows the tail coming together a little odd. The three pieces should join together as one at the same place that it fuses into the tailbone",
      "source": "Nick, this chat",
      "approval": null
    },
    "root-tail-balance": {
      "status": "user-direction",
      "instruction": "Retain centered root overlapping both rear sides; moderate the bulky blend and balance upper/lower reach. Do not leave one elongated lower tail and a short upper tail.",
      "words": "Yeah, I think you overcompensated a tad. Maybe combine those two a little bit so that you still have the tail base being centered, but there's not one longer tail on the bottom and one short tail on top",
      "source": "Nick, this chat",
      "approval": null
    },
    "clean-tail-transition": {
      "status": "user-direction",
      "instruction": "Remove the extra lobe. Center the existing three-tail attachment while preserving a continuous modest lower-back surface; no fourth form at the root.",
      "words": "That last one has like a weird growth Like it's supposed to be the butt cheek muscle, but it got squished in. I think you need to put more effort into the prompt you're giving it",
      "source": "Nick, this chat",
      "approval": null
    },
    "tail-rear-acceptance": {
      "status": "accepted-aspects",
      "instruction": "Study-0018 tail shape and positioning accepted. Preserve unchanged; no whole-stage approval.",
      "words": "this tail and positioning looks good. You're starting to give the creature human-like hands and feet, and that is not going the direction that I originally wanted, but otherwise, the tail looks good",
      "source": "Nick, this chat",
      "evidence": {
        "path": "docs/design/species-construction/akinza/tail-rear-acceptance.json",
        "role": "scoped-acceptance",
        "sha256": "7d79392046c744bb5fae1a0eaba24b2c01851a96fcff06c6ce81e913b1586d06"
      }
    },
    "animal-paws": {
      "status": "user-direction",
      "instruction": "Reject human-like hands and feet. Explore compact clawed animal paws guided by the original silhouette; exact digit counts and foot posture remain provisional.",
      "words": "this tail and positioning looks good. You're starting to give the creature human-like hands and feet, and that is not going the direction that I originally wanted, but otherwise, the tail looks good",
      "source": "Nick, this chat",
      "approval": null
    },
    "paw-acceptance": {
      "status": "accepted-aspects",
      "instruction": "Preserve study-0020 animal-paw direction; full-body scale and limb transition remain provisional.",
      "words": "looks good, proceed. Where are we in the overall layered plan? which layer are we on? I want to make sure we are both following the plan as well as being smart about adjusting the plan where needed, and documenting lessons learned and actually learning from the lessons in the moment",
      "source": "Nick, this chat, responding to animal-paw study-0020",
      "evidence": {
        "path": "docs/design/species-construction/akinza/paw-acceptance.json",
        "role": "scoped-acceptance",
        "sha256": "49120cb27e9a65ab2f76a0d8bcabb226cf944188b26df3c54d78524a36f7f3fb"
      }
    },
    "hind-leg-strength": {
      "status": "user-direction",
      "instruction": "Modestly fuller thighs and upper calves that visually imply bounding strength, preserving the existing creature leg design. Do not replace the legs with literal rabbit anatomy.",
      "words": "The creature's legs became a bit too thin. I wanted the concept of the creature to have a body that was structured closer to a rabbit where the rabbit's hind legs were beefier and intended to provide the bounding strength. To be clear, I don't want you to go all the way to the portion of putting rabbit legs on this creature. I'm just saying what you have here is very close, it's just a bit too thin. I'd prefer the makeup of the legs to imply better strength",
      "source": "Nick, this chat, responding to blockout-0004",
      "evidence": {
        "path": "docs/design/species-construction/akinza/hind-leg-feedback.json",
        "role": "scoped-feedback",
        "sha256": "03dbdcbc73aa370b2ad6dc87a4037fc91dd21dd43cad2b49dc7d068870920416"
      }
    },
    "shin-alignment": {
      "status": "user-direction",
      "instruction": "Retain the improved fullness and thigh-to-knee articulation, but remove the bowed shin. Use a straight underlying knee-to-ankle segment with soft muscle contour and localized joint transitions.",
      "words": "That's a little better, but I also noticed that the leg seems to be very wavy. I think the top part makes sense because it's the thigh going to a knee. So there's a hinge there. So that makes sense how it curves backwards. But then in the middle of the shin, it looks like the shin itself is an arc. I feel like that needs to be reworked a little bit so that the shin looks more reasonable",
      "source": "Nick, this chat, reviewing blockout-0005",
      "evidence": {
        "path": "docs/design/species-construction/akinza/shin-feedback.json",
        "role": "scoped-feedback",
        "sha256": "da43603c8758e18784a577a8c97edb33e203f298b74617cdc4c38182a5454a64"
      }
    },
    "whole-creature-likeness": {
      "status": "user-direction",
      "instruction": "The first grayscale round is the whole-creature likeness target. The current 3D proxy is far from an equivalent untextured model; do not advance on a claim that only fur or texture remains.",
      "words": "Okay, so if you're telling me that fur and surface texture is the only thing that should remain after this pass, then I think we have a LOT more work before that next pass. The first round of grayscale images you produced, I think, look great. And if you had a 3D model that looked just like that, minus the texture, I think we'd be good to go forward. But what you're showing me here is far from an equivalent 3D model aligning to the first round of reference images from the different angles",
      "source": "Nick, this chat, following blockout-0006",
      "evidence": {
        "path": "docs/design/species-construction/akinza/likeness-feedback.json",
        "role": "quality-correction",
        "sha256": "4cd9375ea4ee0d02ad828d4fdf69ec2dc016ddffdaf6150adc1304bead399576"
      }
    }
